![security-critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/security-critical.svg) ![critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/critical.svg)

On 32-bit architectures (such as `armeabi-v7a`), `size_t` is 32-bit. When `config->size.width` and `config->size.height` are close to the maximum allowed limit of `65536.0`, the multiplication `row_bytes * height` (e.g., `262144 * 65536 = 17179869184`) will overflow to `0` in 32-bit unsigned integer arithmetic.

This causes `allocation_size` to be set to `4` (via the `if (allocation_size == 0)` check), leading to a critical heap buffer overflow when the software renderer writes up to 17 GB of pixel data into the 4-byte buffer.

To prevent this vulnerability, perform the multiplication using `uint64_t` and explicitly check for overflow against `std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max()` or enforce a reasonable maximum buffer size limit.

```c
      size_t width = static_cast<size_t>(config->size.width);
      size_t height = static_cast<size_t>(config->size.height);
      uint64_t total_bytes = static_cast<uint64_t>(width) * 4 * height;
      if (total_bytes > std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max() || total_bytes == 0) {
        return false;
      }
      size_t row_bytes = width * 4;
      size_t allocation_size = static_cast<size_t>(total_bytes);
```

